## 0. Context Summary (do not skip — agent should read this fully)

**Current stack:**
- Frontend: React (aashatextile.com), client-rendered SPA
- Backend: Python (FastAPI), hosted on Render (paid tier) — sleeps after ~15 min idle, causing slow first response
- Database: MongoDB
- An earlier version of a "daily drop" concept already exists conceptually (YouTube-linked, TTL-based auto-expiry), but needs to be rebuilt properly as described below.

**Business problem being solved:**
The shop owner (Krishna Sharma) posts a new product-showcase video on YouTube roughly every 2 days. After each video, many customers ask for photos/details of items shown in that specific video — too many to handle manually over WhatsApp/comments.

**What the new system must do:**
1. Detect new YouTube uploads automatically (no manual "add video" step, and no paid API/service).
2. For each detected video, create a "Drop": the video embedded at the top, followed by a product grid (photo + details) for items shown in that video, curated by the admin.
3. Each Drop auto-disappears from the public site **5 days** after being added — fully automatic, no manual action needed.
4. Admin can also **manually delete a Drop early** from the admin panel, any time before the 5 days are up.
5. Whole backend moves off Render (Python) onto **Vercel**, so there's no separate paid always-on server and no cold-sleep delay.

**Target stack after migration:**
- Frontend: same React/Vite app (or Next.js if agent recommends — confirm with user before changing framework)
- Backend: **Next.js API routes** (Node/TypeScript) deployed on Vercel — replacing FastAPI entirely
- Database: MongoDB stays (Atlas, so it's already remote — no change needed there)
- Scheduling: **Vercel Cron** (Hobby plan = max once/day, which is enough since uploads are every ~2 days)
- Auto-expiry: **MongoDB TTL index** on the Drops collection (native Mongo feature, free, no extra cron needed for deletion)

**Constraints:**
- Must stay on free/existing-cost tools — no new paid services.
- Backend logic is simple CRUD, not heavy computation — safe to rewrite in Node.
- User (Tejas) will run each phase through an AI coding agent one at a time, so every phase below must be independently completable and testable.

